---
import ToolLayout from '@/layouts/ToolLayout.astro'
import EmojiSearch from '@/components/tools/reference/EmojiSearch.tsx'
---

<ToolLayout slug="emoji-search">
  <EmojiSearch client:load />

  <Fragment slot="explainer">
    <h2>How to use the emoji search</h2>
    <p>
      Type a word to search emoji names and keywords, or pick a group to browse. Select an emoji to see
      its official name, code points, the Emoji version that added it, its UTF-8 bytes and the HTML
      entity. Tap the large emoji, or use the copy button, to put it on the clipboard.
    </p>
    <h2>Names and keywords</h2>
    <p>
      Each emoji has one short name from the Unicode Consortium, such as "grinning face". The keywords
      come from the English annotations of the Unicode CLDR project, which phone keyboards also use for
      emoji suggestions, so a search for "happy" finds smiling faces too.
    </p>
    <h2>Emoji made of several code points</h2>
    <p>
      Many emoji are sequences: a flag is two regional indicator letters, a family is several people
      joined by zero-width joiners (U+200D), and some symbols need the variation selector U+FE0F to show
      in colour. The code points line lists every part. The list shows each emoji in its default skin
      tone.
    </p>
  </Fragment>
</ToolLayout>
